<p>A Dallas hailstorm can pass in ten minutes and leave a roof that looks fine from the driveway. That is exactly why so many <strong>hail damage roof insurance claims</strong> run into trouble. The homeowner waits, a ceiling stain shows up after the next heavy rain, and by then the insurer wants to know why nobody reported damage from a storm that hit months ago.</p>

<p>This guide walks through how hail actually damages the roofs common in Dallas and the surrounding North Texas cities, how to spot and document it, how to read your roof coverage, what roof estimates tend to miss, and how to protect your roof before the next storm. It is written for homeowners who want to handle the claim well, whether they do it themselves or with help from a Dallas Public Adjuster.</p>

<h2>How often hail hits Dallas County roofs</h2>

<p>North Texas sits in the southern Plains hail belt, and the record shows it. The <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NOAA Storm Events Database</a> lists 841 hail reports for Dallas County since 1955. Of those, 250 were 1.75 inches or larger, and 46 reached 2.75 inches (baseball size) or more. The season is long, but it is not even:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Dallas County hail reports (NOAA)</th><th>What it means for roofs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>March</td><td>105</td><td>Season starts; inspect after early spring storms</td></tr>
<tr><td>April</td><td>241</td><td>Peak month for hail reports</td></tr>
<tr><td>May</td><td>197</td><td>Second-busiest month; storms often pair hail with strong wind</td></tr>
<tr><td>June</td><td>123</td><td>Large-hail days still common</td></tr>
<tr><td>July–September</td><td>71 combined</td><td>Quieter, but not hail-free</td></tr>
<tr><td>October</td><td>61</td><td>Secondary fall bump; easy to forget at claim time</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>A few storms show what is possible. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=386504" target="_blank" rel="noopener">June 13, 2012</a>, baseball-size hail fell for nearly 30 minutes over the Lakewood area of Dallas. NOAA’s report describes roofs damaged so badly that some were left with holes, along with smashed house and car windows. On <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1105878" target="_blank" rel="noopener">June 12, 2023</a>, hail estimated at 4 inches was photographed near Cedar Hill. And on <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1326034" target="_blank" rel="noopener">April 25, 2026</a>, hail up to about 3 inches was reported around Dallas Baptist University, with car windows blown out.</p>

<p>You can check reports near your address with our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> or browse the full <a href="/storm-history/">Dallas storm history</a>.</p>

<h2>How hail damages different roof types</h2>

<p>The size of the stones is only part of the story. Wind speed and direction, the age of the roof, the attic ventilation, and the roofing material all change what an impact does. Here is how the roofs you see across Dallas County tend to respond:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Roof type</th><th>Where you see it</th><th>Typical hail damage</th><th>Claim issue to watch</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Composition (asphalt) shingle</td><td>Most brick-veneer homes, from Lake Highlands ranches to newer subdivisions</td><td>Granule loss, bruised or fractured mat, torn edges, broken seal strips</td><td>Damage is subtle and can be mislabeled as wear</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metal panels and trim</td><td>Some homes, porches, and accent roofs</td><td>Dents in panels and seams, coating damage</td><td>Some policies exclude cosmetic dents on metal; read the endorsement</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clay, concrete, or slate tile</td><td>Some custom and older homes</td><td>Cracked, chipped, or broken pieces</td><td>Matching and underlayment condition drive the scope</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flat or low-slope (TPO, modified bitumen)</td><td>Apartments, offices, retail, and warehouses</td><td>Membrane punctures, fractured surfacing, damaged rooftop units and drains</td><td>Leaks may appear inside before anyone finds the roof damage</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Composition shingles are the main concern for most Dallas homeowners. When hail knocks granules loose or bruises the fiberglass mat, the shingle loses its protection from heat and UV. Dallas summers then speed up the deterioration. A roof that looked “a little scuffed” in May can be shedding granules and leaking by the following spring.</p>

<p>Hail damage also tends to look random: impacts scattered across a slope, heavier on the side that faced the storm. Normal aging tends to be more uniform. That distinction matters because “wear and tear” is one of the most common reasons insurers give for paying less on a roof.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Roof-Insurance-Claim-How-to-Protect-Your-Roof-2-1024x683.png" alt="Adjuster in a cap showing a clipboard to a worried couple in front of a house with a tarped roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Walking the findings through with the homeowner helps make sure nothing on the roof or exterior is left out of the claim.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Signs of hail damage to check after a storm</h2>

<h3>From the ground</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dents or dings in gutters, downspouts, and metal roof vents</li>
<li>Piles of shingle granules at downspout outlets or in splash blocks</li>
<li>Torn window screens, dented garage doors, cracked or chipped siding and trim</li>
<li>Bent fins on the outdoor AC condenser</li>
<li>Spatter marks (clean spots) on fences, mailboxes, or painted surfaces</li>
<li>Damage on neighbors’ roofs and cars, which helps confirm the storm hit your street</li>
</ul>

<h3>On the roof (leave this to a professional)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dark spots where granules have been knocked off</li>
<li>Soft, spongy areas that indicate a bruised mat</li>
<li>Cracks or tears at shingle edges and on ridge caps</li>
<li>Dented or split flashing, pipe jacks, turtle vents, and exhaust caps</li>
</ul>

<h3>Inside the house</h3>
<ul>
<li>New ceiling stains, bubbling paint, or damp drywall</li>
<li>Wet insulation or stained decking in the attic</li>
<li>A musty smell after rain</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to do in the first days after a hailstorm</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay off the roof.</strong> Wet shingles, steep pitches, and two-story drops cause serious injuries. A roofer or adjuster with the right equipment can inspect safely.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph what you can reach safely.</strong> Take wide shots of each side of the house, then close-ups of gutters, vents, siding, windows, the AC unit, and fences. Photos of hailstones next to a coin or ruler are useful.</li>
<li><strong>Write down the storm details.</strong> Date, approximate time, hail size you saw, and how long it lasted. NOAA reports can back this up later.</li>
<li><strong>Stop further damage.</strong> If water is coming in, tarp the roof, move belongings, and keep receipts. Most policies expect you to take reasonable steps to prevent more damage, and TDI notes that reasonable temporary repairs may be covered.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly.</strong> Get the claim number, the adjuster’s name, and the best way to reach them. Put important requests in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Be there for the inspection.</strong> Walk the property with the adjuster, point out everything you found, and ask which slopes and items they are including.</li>
</ol>

<p>For a full list of what to photograph and keep, see <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</a> and our printable <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a>.</p>

