<h2>Understanding your roof coverage before you read the estimate</h2>

<p>The estimate only makes sense once you know what your policy promises. These terms usually decide how much of a hail damage roof insurance claim gets paid:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductible.</strong> Many Texas policies have a separate deductible for wind and hail, often a percentage of your dwelling (Coverage A) limit. A 2% deductible on a $350,000 dwelling limit is $7,000, which comes off the top of the payment.</li>
<li><strong>Replacement cost value (RCV).</strong> Pays what it costs to replace the roof at today’s prices. Insurers typically pay the actual cash value first and release the withheld (recoverable) depreciation after the work is done, often within a deadline in the policy.</li>
<li><strong>Actual cash value (ACV).</strong> Replacement cost minus depreciation. TDI notes that some insurers switch older roofs to ACV coverage at renewal, so check for changes each year.</li>
<li><strong>Roof payment schedules and cosmetic exclusions.</strong> Some policies pay a percentage of the roof cost based on age, and some exclude dents on metal that do not affect function. Look for these in the endorsements.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law coverage.</strong> Pays, up to its limit, for the extra cost of meeting building codes when repairing covered damage.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> shows how the deductible and depreciation affect what you actually receive.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Roof-Insurance-Claim-How-to-Protect-Your-Roof-3-1024x683.png" alt="Adjuster at a desk reviewing roof photos on a tablet with a hard hat nearby" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Comparing labeled roof photos with the insurer’s estimate, line by line, is where missed items usually show up.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What first roof estimates commonly miss</h2>

<p>After a big North Texas storm, adjusters may be handling heavy caseloads. Some inspections are quick, and some are done partly from the ground or by drone. Items that often deserve a second look include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Accurate roof measurements, pitch, and the waste factor for cutting shingles</li>
<li>Tear-off of additional layers if the roof has been overlaid</li>
<li>Starter course, hip and ridge cap, and drip edge</li>
<li>Step flashing, counter flashing, pipe jacks, and roof vents</li>
<li>Underlayment, and any decking replacement found during tear-off</li>
<li>Steep-roof and high-roof labor charges on two-story homes</li>
<li>Gutters, downspouts, screens, siding, fences, and the AC condenser</li>
<li>Permit fees and code-required items, where the policy covers them</li>
</ul>

<p>Re-roofing in the City of Dallas generally requires a permit through the City of Dallas Planning &amp; Development Department. Suburbs such as Richardson, Garland, Irving, and Plano have their own permit offices. Code requirements do not automatically mean the insurer pays for upgrades. That depends on your ordinance or law coverage, which our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page explains in more detail.</p>

<h2>Repair or replace? How the decision is usually made</h2>

<p>The most common disagreement on a roof claim is whether the roof should be patched or replaced. Policy language controls, but these factors usually drive the answer:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>How many slopes are damaged.</strong> Insurers often evaluate each slope separately. Consistent hail hits on every storm-facing slope is a different situation from a handful of isolated impacts.</li>
<li><strong>Whether repair is practical.</strong> Older shingles can become brittle and crack when a roofer lifts them to slip in new ones. If that happens, ask the roofer to document it with photos or video.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> A discontinued shingle or faded color may make a patch obvious. How matching is handled depends on the policy wording.</li>
<li><strong>Pre-existing wear.</strong> TDI is clear that insurers will not pay for a new roof just because the old one is worn out. Clear evidence that the fractures and bruises are fresh and storm-related is what supports the claim.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to expect on timing</h2>

<p>Texas’s Prompt Payment of Claims law (Insurance Code Chapter 542) generally gives the insurer 15 days after notice to acknowledge the claim, begin its investigation, and request information. Once it has everything it asked for, it generally has 15 business days to accept or reject the claim, or it can tell you it needs more time, up to 45 more days. Payment is generally due within 5 business days after it accepts. After a declared weather catastrophe, these deadlines can be extended by 15 days. Keep a simple log of dates, calls, and documents so you always know where the claim stands.</p>

<h2>Supplements: how a roof estimate gets corrected</h2>

<p>A supplement is a documented request to add missed, under-measured, or newly discovered items to the insurer’s estimate. It stays under the same claim. Hidden problems such as soft or broken decking often appear only during tear-off. When that happens, photograph the condition before it is covered up, notify the insurer right away, and give it a chance to inspect.</p>

<p>Strong supplements include photos, measurements, a short explanation for each item, and a line-item estimate. Our guide to <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements-what-carriers-miss-in-their-scope/">hail damage claim supplements</a> covers the process in depth.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Roof-Insurance-Claim-How-to-Protect-Your-Roof-4-1024x683.png" alt="House with a blue tarp on the roof, a ladder against the wall, and a lawn covered in hailstones" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Tarping a damaged roof right away helps prevent further damage; keep the receipts for the claim.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Common mistakes that weaken roof claims</h2>
<ul>
<li>Waiting months to report damage after a known storm</li>
<li>Making permanent repairs before the insurer has inspected</li>
<li>Accepting the first estimate without comparing it with a roofer’s scope</li>
<li>Signing a contractor agreement that says the contractor will “handle the insurance claim”</li>
<li>Accepting an offer to waive or cover your deductible, which TDI says is illegal in Texas</li>
<li>Losing track of letters, emails, photos, and estimates</li>
</ul>

<h2>Protecting your roof before the next storm season</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Take dated photos</strong> of the roof each year before March. Before-and-after images help show what a storm changed.</li>
<li><strong>Have the roof inspected</strong> every few years and after any significant hail. TDI recommends hiring a roof inspector periodically to catch problems early.</li>
<li><strong>Keep gutters clean</strong> and trim branches that hang over the roof.</li>
<li><strong>Consider impact-resistant materials</strong> when you replace the roof, and ask your agent about any discount.</li>
<li><strong>Review your policy at renewal</strong> for changes to roof coverage, deductibles, or ACV terms.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to get help with a roof claim</h2>

<p>Plenty of roof claims are settled between the homeowner, the roofer, and the insurer without trouble. A public adjuster may be worth considering when the damage is extensive, the estimate is far below your roofer’s scope, slopes were left out or denied, or the claim has stalled. A Dallas Public Adjuster reviews the policy, documents the roof and exterior, writes a line-item estimate, and presents it to the insurer on your behalf. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<p>Learn more about our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claim help</a> and <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> service, available to homeowners in Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities.</p>
